## Reasoning summary

**Wiring decisions**

| Factory | Registration |
|---|---|
| `UnitOfWork` | `Map<Schema.SObjectType, List<Schema.SObjectField>>` for `Account`, `Contact`, `Opportunity`, `OpportunityContactRole` — includes the FK/relationship fields (`Contact.AccountId`, `Opportunity.AccountId`, `OpportunityContactRole.OpportunityId`, `OpportunityContactRole.ContactId`) so `registerNew`/`registerRelationship` can wire new Accounts → new Contacts → new Opportunities → new OpportunityContactRoles in a single `commitWork()`. |
| `Service` | `Map<System.Type, System.Type>` mapping the `IAccountsService` interface to its `AccountsServiceImpl` implementation (default constructor, so the factory can instantiate it). |
| `Selector` | `Map<Schema.SObjectType, System.Type>` mapping `Account → AccountsSelector`, `Contact → ContactsSelector` (so `Application.Selector.newInstance(Account.SObjectType)` can be cast to `AccountsSelector`). |
| `Domain` | `Map<Schema.SObjectType, System.Type>` mapping `Account → Accounts.Constructor` (an `fflib_SObjectDomain.IConstructable`), constructed with the **same** `Selector` factory instance declared above, so `Application.Domain.newInstance(Set<Id>)` resolves the records through the (mockable) selector factory rather than a private one. |

**Ordering:** Apex initializes static fields in declaration order, so `Selector` is declared before `Domain` and can safely be passed into the domain factory.

**Other notes:** the class is `inherited sharing` (composition root, never the owner of a transaction boundary beyond delegation); nothing else is instantiated eagerly, and no record data is queried at class-initialization time — the domain only queries when `newInstance(Set<Id>)` is called.

