/**
 * AccountTrigger (after insert)
 * ============================
 *
 * Pushes every newly created Account to the ERP as a customer.
 *
 * Why this design
 * ---------------
 * 1. A trigger is executed once per transaction, and Trigger.new then contains
 *    ALL accounts created in that transaction (1 account for an interactive
 *    create, up to 200 for the marketplace integration). The trigger therefore
 *    hands the complete list to ErpCustomerSync - it never looks at
 *    Trigger.new[0] only - so no account is silently skipped.
 *
 * 2. The ERP endpoint would also accept up to 200 customers in a single request,
 *    but the ERP team wants one request per customer so that every account can
 *    be traced on its own. ErpCustomerSync therefore sends exactly one POST per
 *    account, each with a one element JSON array as body.
 *
 * 3. DEVIATION (documented on purpose): a single Salesforce transaction may
 *    contain at most 100 callouts. The marketplace integration creates up to 200
 *    accounts in one transaction, so pushing all of them synchronously from
 *    this trigger would raise System.LimitException and roll the whole insert
 *    back. Batches with more than 100 accounts are therefore handed over to an
 *    asynchronous job (ErpCustomerSync.ErpCustomerSyncJob) that chains itself,
 *    so every transaction stays within the limit. The 1:1 relation between
 *    created accounts and ERP requests is unchanged, and for the normal case
 *    (a user creating a single account) the request is still sent synchronously
 *    from this trigger, so the user immediately sees an error if the ERP does
 *    not accept the account.
 *
 * 4. There is no DML in or after the callout path, which keeps us clear of the
 *    "You have uncommitted work pending" error that occurs when DML follows a
 *    callout in the same transaction.
 */
trigger AccountTrigger on Account (after insert) {
    if (Trigger.isAfter && Trigger.isInsert) {
        ErpCustomerSync.pushAccounts(Trigger.new);
    }
}
