## What changed and why

`orgPreferences` is a legacy, org-creation-only block (2019-era "org preferences" API). It is no longer accepted by `sf org create scratch` on current releases, and it is not expressible as a normal source file. The supported equivalent is the **`settings`** block in the scratch org definition file, whose contents are the **Metadata API `Settings`** type (child settings types → their fields). The CLI applies that block as a `Settings` metadata deployment immediately after the org is created, so the org itself is created cleanly and the preferences are then set.

Mapping used (all five preferences, enabled *and* disabled, are expressed):

| Legacy `orgPreferences` | Metadata API setting used |
|---|---|
| `S1DesktopEnabled` (on) | `lightningExperienceSettings.enableS1DesktopEnabled = true` – keeps Salesforce Classic available, as the 2019 org had |
| `ChatterEnabled` (on) | `chatterSettings.enableChatter = true` |
| `NetworksEnabled` (on) | `networksSettings.enableNetworks = true` (Experience Cloud networks; reinforced by the retained `Communities` feature) |
| `Translation` (on) | `translationSettings.translationEnabled = true` – Translation Workbench / platform translation on |
| `S1EncryptedStoragePref2` (**off**) | `mobileSettings.enableS1EncryptedStoragePref2 = false` – explicitly disabled, matching the `disabled` list |

Notes / assumptions:
- `orgName`, `edition` (Developer) and `features` (`Communities`) are preserved verbatim; `orgName` with a space is fine for the definition file (it is not a Salesforce object name).
- On today's releases Chatter and Networks/Communities are always on in a scratch org, so those two entries are belt-and-braces; if your org's policies ever reject them, delete just those two entries — the rest of the file still creates without error. Everything else is a straight settings deployment.
- If you later need to keep these settings under source control, run `sf project retrieve start --metadata Settings` (or pull the org settings) and the same values will land in `settings/*.settings-meta.xml` — the definition file and the source-format file carry identical values.

