## Reasoning

**What the definition file can do:**

| Requirement | How |
|---|---|
| Developer edition + org name | `edition` / `orgName` top‑level keys |
| State & Country/territory picklists | `features`: `StatePicklist`, `CountryPicklist` (these are what create `BillingCountryCode`, `BillingStateCode`, `MailingCountryCode`, etc.) |
| Contacts to Multiple Accounts | `features`: `MultipleContactsToAccounts` |

**What it cannot do (handled post‑create):**

*Standard sample data.* Built‑in sample data is no longer something the org‑creation definition can request — on the current release the scratch org is created empty and the sample data (accounts, contacts, opportunities) must be loaded afterwards (e.g. by installing the Salesforce sample‑data package or seeding it with a script). Putting a guessed `"name": "SampleData"`/`"Sample Data"` entry in `features` would make `sf org create scratch` fail with an invalid‑feature error, so per the instruction I left it out rather than guess — the post‑create script will install it.

I also deliberately kept the file minimal (no `settings` keys, no admin permissions, no `sourceApiVersion`/`namespace` keys) so that `sf org create scratch --definition-file config/project-scratch-def.json` creates the org cleanly on the current release. Note that picklists being enabled makes the `...CountryCode`/`...StateCode` fields available, and the ERP middleware can then populate them (values must be the picklist API names, e.g. `US`/`CA`).

